    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Beaumont is the Jefferson County seat and the largest city in the Golden Triangle, about 20 miles north of Port Arthur. If your Beaumont home, rental, or business has been damaged by a hurricane, a tropical downpour, a severe thunderstorm, a fire, or a burst pipe, a public adjuster in Beaumont can help you document the loss, read your policies, and present the claim to your insurer. Port Arthur Public Adjuster serves Beaumont as part of our core Jefferson County service area.</p>

<h2>Beaumont's storm exposure</h2>
<p>Beaumont sits inland from the Gulf, but distance from the shore has not protected it from tropical weather. The biggest Beaumont claims in recent memory have come from rain and rising water rather than surge.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hurricane Harvey (2017).</strong> According to the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service in Lake Charles</a>, Jefferson County saw widespread storm totals over 40 inches between August 24 and 31, 2017. The northeast side of Beaumont was listed among the hardest-hit areas, and record flood crests were observed on Pine Island Bayou and the lower Neches River. More than 64,000 homes flooded across the county.</li>
<li><strong>Tropical Storm Imelda (2019).</strong> Imelda brought extreme rainfall and widespread flash flooding to Southeast Texas in September 2019, including the Beaumont area, only two years after Harvey.</li>
<li><strong>Hurricanes Rita (2005), Humberto (2007), and Ike (2008).</strong> Rita caused widespread wind damage across Southeast Texas, and Humberto caused wind damage in the Port Arthur and Beaumont area. Ike's wind damage reached across the region.</li>
<li><strong>Hurricane Laura (2020).</strong> NWS Lake Charles reported numerous trees, power lines, and poles blown down in Jefferson County, with some homes and businesses damaged by fallen trees or wind.</li>
</ul>
<p>Beyond named storms, Beaumont gets severe thunderstorms with straight-line winds, occasional hail, and the occasional hard freeze like February 2021. You can search reports near any Beaumont ZIP code with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>, or browse the full <a href="/storm-history/">Jefferson County storm history</a>.</p>

<h2>Insurance realities for Beaumont property owners</h2>
<h3>Wind coverage and TWIA</h3>
<p>Because Jefferson County is a first-tier coastal county in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) catastrophe area, some Beaumont owners buy wind and hail coverage from TWIA while their homeowners policy excludes it. Others have wind covered by a private insurer. The difference matters: TWIA claims have their own reporting deadline, decision timeline, and appraisal rules, and roof repairs generally need TDI windstorm inspection and a WPI-8 certificate to keep the property eligible. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page explains the process.</p>

<h3>Flood is separate</h3>
<p>Harvey and Imelda flooded many Beaumont homes that were not in a high-risk flood zone. Standard homeowners, commercial, and TWIA policies generally exclude flood, so rising-water damage goes to an NFIP or private flood policy. When a property has both wind and flood damage, the claims are usually split by cause, each with its own adjuster and deductible. Clear photos of water lines and roof damage help keep that split accurate. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Deductibles</h3>
<p>Many Texas policies set wind/hail or named-storm deductibles as a percentage of the dwelling limit. If you are not sure what you would owe out of pocket in a hurricane, try the <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Beaumont property types we see</h2>
<p>Beaumont has a wide mix of construction. Many older neighborhoods have pier-and-beam or slab homes with composition shingle roofs and wood, vinyl, fiber-cement, or brick veneer siding; newer subdivisions are mostly slab-on-grade. Commercial corridors include strip centers, offices, churches, and warehouses, and the industrial areas along the Neches River include metal buildings and low-slope roofs. Each type fails differently in a storm:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pier-and-beam homes</strong> can take water damage in crawlspaces, subfloors, and ductwork that is easy to overlook.</li>
<li><strong>Slab homes</strong> that flood often need drywall, insulation, flooring, cabinets, and doors replaced to a consistent height.</li>
<li><strong>Metal and low-slope commercial roofs</strong> can suffer seam, fastener, and membrane damage from wind that is hard to see from the ground.</li>
</ul>
<p>Learn more on our <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> pages.</p>

